<title>JAPAN: Sumitomo has no comment on copper report.</title>
<headline>Sumitomo has no comment on copper report.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1996-11-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>Sumitomo Corp has no comment on a report that huge unrecorded copper stocks may be sitting in Rotterdam or on whether such stocks might have been connected to Sumitomo's former chief copper trader Yasuo Hamanaka, a spokesman said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;In view of the influence on the market, we do not comment at all on our inventories and market positions,&quot; the spokesman said.</p>
<p>On Wednesday, the World Bureau of Metal Statistics said in a monthly report that based on data showing copper receipts far exceeding deliveries, an unrecorded stockpile of more than 500,000 tonnes of copper may have been built up in Netherlands bonded warehouses from 1990 through 1995.</p>
<p>Traders have speculated that Hamanaka, who was fired by Sumitomo in June for alleged unauthorised trading, maintained a grip on large physical stocks to drive copper prices higher.</p>
<p>-- Tokyo Commodity Desk +81-3-3432-6179</p>
